-- je54-nouns: the nouns of the Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954 (L.17/1954), in every deposited version.  DECLARE and nothing else.
-- Every other module of this row imports this one.  The membership test for a noun: could a witness of ordinary competence testify to the fact, or does answering it need the Law?
-- Only the first kind is here.  Rules, tables of data and tests live in the other modules.
--
-- The seat is the reader of an enactment: someone who has to know what an enactment means for them, when it bound them, what may be done to them, and whether a notice reached them in time.
-- Facts are ones that person could state.  The reading chosen at each fork (NOTES.md section 6) is an enumeration here, taken as a TYPICALLY default by the function that reads it.

§ `Which text of the Law`

-- The nine answers to "which text of the Law applies on this day?".  Seven are deposited texts (V1, then V2a to V2f; the letters follow the vintage table in NOTES.md section 5).  Two are not texts:
-- the days from 28 March 2003 to 31 December 2018 (when only four provisions may be answered, by inference from the endnotes of the 2019 consolidation) and the days before 28 March 2003.
DECLARE `Text of the Law` IS ONE OF
    `no text before 28 March 2003`
    `the inferred text from 28 March 2003 to 31 December 2018`
    `V1, 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021`
    `V2a, 28 September 2021 to 30 January 2022`
    `V2b, 31 January 2022 to 31 August 2022`
    `V2c, 1 September 2022 to 10 January 2024`
    `V2d, 11 January 2024 to 23 November 2025`
    `V2e, 24 November 2025 to 18 June 2026`
    `V2f, 19 June 2026 to current`

-- One row of the vintage table: a text of the Law, the days it governs, the deposited file that shows it and the amendment that began it.
DECLARE `Row of the vintage table` HAS
    `which text` IS A `Text of the Law` @desc The text of the Law that the row describes
    `first day` IS A DATE @desc The first day on which the text is in force
    `last day` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc The last day on which the text is in force; NOTHING for the text in force now
    `source stem` IS A STRING @desc The stem of the deposited file that shows the text, for example l_17_1954_20210927; the file is SOURCES/ stem .txt
    `amendment that began it` IS A STRING @desc The amendment, or the event, that made this text the text in force, as the endnotes name it

-- How sure the encoding is of an answer.  The four provisions that the endnotes of the 2019 consolidation date to the Interpretation (Amendment) (Jersey) Law 2003 are answered for the days
-- from 28 March 2003 to 31 December 2018 by inference only.  The third marks a day found under the text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021 that falls on or after 28 September 2021: from then the commencement of an
-- enactment is governed by the Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021, which this row does not encode; that Law gives the same day for a named day (checked by the integrator on 2026-10-28 against Article 3(2), subject je/legislation-2021, source/l_8_2021.current.txt line 48).
DECLARE `Basis of a reading` IS ONE OF
    `read from the deposited text in force on that day`
    `inferred from the endnotes of the 2019 consolidation, not verified against L.16/2003`
    `governed by the 2021 Law, Article 3(2)`

§ `Article 1: the instrument, and the time of its commencement`

-- Article 1(1): the kinds of instrument that the Law calls an enactment.
DECLARE `Kind of instrument` IS ONE OF
    `a Law passed by the States and confirmed in Council`
    `regulations, an Order, rules, bye-laws, a scheme or other instrument passed or made in Jersey under the authority of an Order in Council`
    `regulations, an Order, rules, bye-laws, a scheme or other instrument passed or made in Jersey under a Law passed by the States and confirmed in Council`
    `something that is none of these`

-- Article 1(2) (V1 only), fork I3: what the Article does for the day on which a Law is deemed passed.
DECLARE `Reading of Article 1(2)` IS ONE OF
    `the Law is deemed passed on the later of the day it was confirmed and the day it was registered`
    `the Article only bars an earlier day: the caller states the day`

-- Article 1(3): the words the Article reads differently.
DECLARE `Word read by Article 1(3)` IS ONE OF
    `passing`
    `repeal`

DECLARE `Reading under Article 1(3)` IS ONE OF
    `as a reference to the making of the enactment`
    `as a reference to the rescission of the enactment`
    `as written`

-- Articles 1(4) to 1(7): how an enactment states the time at which it is to come into operation.
DECLARE `How an enactment expresses the time of its commencement` IS ONE OF
    `on a particular day`
    `forthwith`
    `silent as to the time at which it is to come into operation`
    `calculated by reference to its promulgation`

-- Fork I1: which day selects the text of the Law that is read for the commencement of an enactment.  The first reading (taken) finds the commencement under the text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021 and asks whether it
-- came before 28 September 2021; the second and third are the text in force on a day.
DECLARE `Which day selects the text for commencement` IS ONE OF
    `whether the enactment had come into force before 28 September 2021`
    `the day the enactment was passed or made`
    `the day on which the question about the enactment is asked`

-- An enactment's commencement as the enactment states it, and the days the question needs.
DECLARE `Commencement as stated` HAS
    `how it is expressed` IS A `How an enactment expresses the time of its commencement` @desc How the enactment states the time at which it is to come into operation
    `the day it names` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc The particular day on which the enactment says it comes into force; NOTHING if it names none
    `the day it was passed or made` IS A DATE @desc The day the enactment was passed or made (for a Law in the text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021, the day it was deemed passed under Article 1(2))
    `the day the question is asked` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc The day on which you are asking; read only if you choose the reading that the text in force on that day governs; NOTHING otherwise

-- Answers that carry how sure they are.
DECLARE `Day with its basis` HAS
    `the day` IS A DATE @desc The day
    `the basis` IS A `Basis of a reading` @desc Whether the answer is read from a deposited text or inferred from the endnotes

DECLARE `Yes or no with its basis` HAS
    `the answer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The answer
    `the basis` IS A `Basis of a reading` @desc Whether the answer is read from a deposited text or inferred from the endnotes

DECLARE `Moment with its basis` HAS
    `the moment` IS A DATETIME @desc The moment
    `the basis` IS A `Basis of a reading` @desc Whether the answer is read from a deposited text or inferred from the endnotes

-- A moment read on a clock, in the time zone in which the day is read.  The Law does not name a time zone (NOTES.md section 7); this row assumes Jersey local time.
DECLARE `Local moment` HAS
    `calendar day` IS A DATE @desc The calendar day on the clock in the time zone in which days are read (Jersey local time is assumed by this row, not stated by the Law)
    `hour of the day` IS A NUMBER @desc The hour on that clock, 0 to 23
    `minute of the hour` IS A NUMBER @desc The minute on that clock, 0 to 59
    `second of the minute` IS A NUMBER @desc The second on that clock, 0 to 59

§ `Articles 4 and 5, the Schedule`

-- How a definition of Part 1 of the Schedule is introduced.  "shall mean" and "means" give a meaning that is complete (exhaustive); "includes", "shall include" and "shall be construed as including" add to the word's
-- own meaning and do not exhaust it; one definition (Magistrate) does both ("shall mean ... and includes ...").
DECLARE `Form of a definition` IS ONE OF
    `means`
    `includes`
    `means and includes`

-- One definition of Part 1 of the Schedule.
DECLARE `Defined expression` HAS
    term IS A STRING @desc The defined word, exactly as the Schedule prints it
    form IS A `Form of a definition` @desc Whether the Schedule says means, includes, or both
    `the condition it states` IS A MAYBE STRING @desc The condition the definition states for itself (in relation to a day; in the case of a person allowed to affirm), if any
    `the things it names` IS A LIST OF STRING @desc The things the definition names or describes, each as the Schedule words it
    `the Schedule's words` IS A STRING @desc The words of the Schedule after the defined word, exactly as it prints them

-- Fork I6: a definition that both means and includes (Magistrate).
DECLARE `Reading of a definition that both means and includes` IS ONE OF
    `the named things are all that the word covers`
    `the named things do not exhaust the word`

DECLARE `Definition with its basis` HAS
    definition IS A `Defined expression` @desc The definition
    `the basis` IS A `Basis of a reading` @desc Whether the definition is read from a deposited text or inferred from the endnotes

-- What a definition does for a thing asked about.
DECLARE `Reach of a defined expression` IS ONE OF
    `within the word, as the Schedule says`
    `outside the word, because the Schedule says means`
    `not named by the Schedule, which says includes and so does not shut it out: the word's own meaning decides`
    `the definition does not apply: the condition it states is not met`

-- Articles 2, 3 and 5.
DECLARE `Kind of word under Article 2` IS ONE OF
    `a word importing the masculine gender`
    `a word importing the feminine gender`
    `a word in the singular`
    `a word in the plural`
    `a grammatical variation of a word to which a definition is assigned`

DECLARE `Reach of a word under Article 2` IS ONE OF
    `the word includes females`
    `the word includes the plural`
    `the word includes the singular`
    `the word is construed in accordance with the definition`

DECLARE `Kind of body` IS ONE OF
    `a body corporate`
    `a body of persons that is unincorporated`

-- Fork I5: the proviso to Article 3 says "nothing in this Article".
DECLARE `Reach of the proviso to Article 3` IS ONE OF
    `the proviso limits Article 3 only`
    `the proviso also limits the Schedule definition of person`

DECLARE `How person reaches a body` IS ONE OF
    `by Article 3 and by the Schedule`
    `by Article 3 only`
    `by the Schedule only`
    `not at all`

§ `Article 7: service by post`

-- What happened to a document that an enactment authorizes or requires to be served by post.  The time at which the letter would be delivered in the ordinary course of post is the caller's fact: neither Law says how
-- long post takes or what the ordinary course of post is (NOTES.md section 7).
DECLARE `Service by post as it happened` HAS
    `the enactment authorizes or requires the document to be served by post` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an enactment authorizes or requires this document to be served by post, whatever word it uses (serve, give, send or another)
    `the letter was properly addressed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the letter containing the document was properly addressed
    `the letter was prepaid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the postage on the letter was prepaid
    `the letter was posted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the letter was posted
    `the time at which the letter would be delivered in the ordinary course of post` IS A `Local moment` @desc The time at which the letter would have been delivered in the ordinary course of post, on a clock in the time zone in which you read days
    `the other time proved` IS A MAYBE `Local moment` @desc A different time at which service is proved to have been effected, on the same clock; NOTHING if nobody has proved one

-- Fork I4: what governs when the contrary is proved.
DECLARE `What governs once the presumption of Article 7 is rebutted` IS ONE OF
    `the time that is proved`
    `the Article gives no time`

§ `Article 8: bankruptcy`

-- The events that Article 8 lists, described by what happened (the letter of the limb is in the @ref: the bank limb is (f) in V2b and (g) from V2c).
DECLARE `Event in the life of a person` IS ONE OF
    `the Royal Court grants the person's own application to place the person's property under the control of the Court`
    `the person's property is declared en désastre`
    `the Viscount grants a debt remission order in respect of the person's qualifying debts under the Debt Remission (Individuals) (Jersey) Law 2016`
    `the Royal Court grants the person's own application to make a general cession of the person's property`
    `the Royal Court adjudges the person's property to be renounced`
    `a company is wound up by means of a creditors' winding up pursuant to the Companies (Jersey) Law 1991`
    `an administration order is made in respect of a company under Part 20B of the Companies (Jersey) Law 1991`
    `a limited liability company is wound up by means of a creditors' winding up under the Limited Liability Companies (Winding Up and Dissolution) (Jersey) Regulations 2022`
    `a bank winding up order is made under the Bank (Recovery and Resolution) (Jersey) Law 2017`
    `some other event`

-- What the person is, for the limbs that begin "in the case of a ...".  The Law does not say whether a limited liability company or a bank is also a "company"; the caller says what the person is.
DECLARE `Person who may have become bankrupt` HAS
    `is a company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is a company within the Companies (Jersey) Law 1991
    `is a limited liability company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is a limited liability company
    `is a bank` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is a bank

-- Fork I2: which text of Article 8 governs.
DECLARE `Which day selects the text for Article 8` IS ONE OF
    `the day of the event`
    `the day on which the question about the event is asked`

§ `Articles 9 and 10: references to enactments, and subordinate enactments`

-- The divisions that Article 9(1) and 9(2) read: a Part, Article or Schedule (9(1)); a paragraph, sub-paragraph, clause or sub-clause (9(2)).
DECLARE `Kind of division referred to` IS ONE OF
    `a Part`
    `an Article`
    `a Schedule`
    `a paragraph`
    `a sub-paragraph`
    `a clause`
    `a sub-clause`

-- A reference as someone reading the enactment sees it.
DECLARE `Reference by number or letter` HAS
    `the kind of division named` IS A `Kind of division referred to` @desc The kind of division the reference names, for example an Article
    `the number or letter given` IS A STRING @desc The number or letter the reference gives, for example 5 or (a)
    `further identification is given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the reference says more than the number or letter, for example by naming another Law
    `the kind of instrument that contains the reference` IS A `Kind of instrument` @desc The kind of instrument in which the reference appears
    `the instrument that contains the reference` IS A STRING @desc The name of the instrument in which the reference appears
    `the division that contains the reference` IS A STRING @desc The Article or other division of that instrument in which the reference appears, for example Article 5

-- Where the reference leads.
DECLARE `Place referred to` HAS
    `the kind of division` IS A `Kind of division referred to` @desc The kind of division the reference leads to
    `the number or letter` IS A STRING @desc The number or letter of that division
    `the place it is in` IS A STRING @desc The instrument, or the Article or other division of it, that the division is in

-- Article 9(3): the forms in which a reference to an enactment may mean the enactment.
DECLARE `Form in which an enactment is meant` IS ONE OF
    `the enactment as amended`
    `the enactment as revised or otherwise consolidated`
    `the enactment as extended or applied under another enactment`
    `the enactment as extended or applied under another provision of the same Law`

-- Article 9(4) and 9(5): how Article 9 applies to an instrument.
DECLARE `How Article 9 applies to an instrument` IS ONE OF
    `as it stands, the instrument being a Law`
    `with the necessary changes, the instrument being a subordinate enactment`

§ `Articles 11 and 11A: powers, duties and appointments`

-- Article 11(3) (V1 only): what a power to pass an enactment is construed as including.
DECLARE `Act on an enactment` IS ONE OF
    `to repeal it`
    `to rescind it`
    `to amend it`
    `to vary it`
    `some other act on it`

-- Article 11(4) (V1 only): the ways in which a power to make an enactment may be exercised.  The paragraphs are 11(4)(a), then 11(4)(b)(i) to (iii).
DECLARE `Way a power to make an enactment is exercised` IS ONE OF
    `in relation to all cases to which the power extends`
    `in relation to all those cases subject to specified exceptions`
    `in relation to any specified cases or classes of case`
    `so as to make the full provision to which the power extends`
    `so as to make any lesser provision, whether by way of exception or otherwise`
    `so as to make the same provision for all cases in which the power is exercised`
    `so as to make different provision for different cases or classes of case`
    `so as to make different provision as respects the same case or class of case for different purposes of the enactment`
    `so as to make any such provision unconditionally`
    `so as to make any such provision subject to a specified condition`
    `in some other way`

-- Article 11A (V2a to V2f): the powers that a power to appoint to an office includes.
DECLARE `Power related to an appointment` IS ONE OF
    `to determine the terms and conditions of appointment`
    `to remove or suspend a person from the office`
    `to reappoint or reinstate a person to the office`
    `to appoint a person to the office in place of the last holder of the office`
    `some other power`

-- Article 11A(2): the circumstances in which the power to appoint in place of the last holder is included.
DECLARE `Circumstances of an office` HAS
    `the office is vacant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the office is vacant
    `the person holding the office has been removed or suspended from office` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person holding the office has been removed or suspended from it
    `the person holding the office is absent temporarily or permanently` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person holding the office is absent, for a time or for good
    `the person holding the office is incapacitated in a way that affects the person's performance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person holding the office is incapacitated, for a time or for good, in a way that affects the person's performance

§ `Articles 12 to 15: offences, penalties, forms and distances`

-- Article 12: the offences that one act or omission constitutes.  An enactment named twice is two offences under one enactment, which Article 12 says nothing about.
-- Article 1(1) defines an "enactment" as any provision of any Law (or of an instrument made under one), so the caller names the provision, not the Law: Articles 21 and 23 of one Law are two enactments.
DECLARE `Offences arising from one act or omission` HAS
    `the enactments that create the offences` IS A LIST OF STRING @desc One entry for each offence under an enactment that the act or omission constitutes, naming the provision that creates it (Article 1(1): an enactment is any provision of any Law, so two Articles of one Law are two enactments); an enactment named twice creates two offences
    `it is also an offence under the customary law of Jersey` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the same act or omission is also an offence under the customary law of Jersey

-- Article 13(2): how the fine for an offence is specified.
DECLARE `How a fine is specified` IS ONE OF
    `an amount is specified`
    `a level on the standard scale is specified`
    `neither an amount nor a level on the standard scale is specified`

-- Article 13(3): how several penalties may be imposed.
DECLARE `Mode of imposing several penalties` IS ONE OF
    `alternatively`
    `cumulatively`

-- Article 14: what a deviation from a prescribed form does.
DECLARE `Effect of a deviation from a form` IS ONE OF
    `the deviation does not invalidate the form used`
    `Article 14 does not save the form used, and does not say that it is invalid`

-- Article 15: how a distance was measured.  The Law gives a method and no formula, so the method is the noun and the measurement is an input.
DECLARE `Way a distance was measured` IS ONE OF
    `in a straight line on a horizontal plane`
    `in another way`

DECLARE `Distance as measured` HAS
    `the distance` IS A NUMBER @desc The distance, in the unit the enactment uses
    `the unit` IS A STRING @desc The unit in which the distance is given, for example metres
    `the way it was measured` IS A `Way a distance was measured` @desc Whether the distance was measured in a straight line on a horizontal plane, or in another way

§ `Articles 6, 16 and 17 (V1 only)`

-- Article 16(1) and 16(2): the instruments that Article 16 deals with.
DECLARE `Instrument dealt with by Article 16` IS ONE OF
    `a Law passed after the commencement of the Law`
    `Regulations passed by the States`
    `some other instrument`

-- Article 17(2)(a) to (e): what a repeal does not do.
DECLARE `Effect of a repeal` IS ONE OF
    `reviving anything not in force or not existing at the time at which the repeal takes effect`
    `affecting the previous operation of the enactment repealed or anything duly done or suffered under it`
    `affecting any right, privilege, obligation or liability acquired, accrued or incurred under the enactment repealed`
    `affecting any penalty, forfeiture or punishment incurred in respect of any offence committed against the enactment repealed`
    `affecting any investigation, legal proceedings or remedy in respect of any such right, privilege, obligation, liability, penalty, forfeiture or punishment`
    `some other effect`

-- Article 17(2), closing words: what may be done as if the repealing enactment had not been passed.
DECLARE `Thing done as if the repealing enactment had not been passed` IS ONE OF
    `instituting, continuing or enforcing an investigation, legal proceeding or remedy`
    `imposing a penalty, forfeiture or punishment`
    `something else`

-- Article 17(3): how the enactment ceased.
DECLARE `How an enactment ceased` IS ONE OF
    `the enactment was repealed`
    `the enactment expired`
    `the enactment ceased in some other way`

§ `What the Law does not say`

-- The units of a period that a reader might ask about.  The Law counts none of them (NOTES.md section 7).
DECLARE `Unit of a period` IS ONE OF
    `a number of days`
    `a number of weeks`
    `a number of years`
